"100 Fastest-Growing Companies", "year": 2025, "rank": 47, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2025, "rank": 470, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "future", "title": "The Future 50", "year": 2024, "rank": 38, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2024, "rank": 512, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2023, "rank": null, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "future", "title": "The Future 50", "year": 2023, "rank": 22, "itemDescription": "Palo Alto Networks is one of the biggest \u201cpure play\u201d companies in cybersecurity, and experts in the industry say it\u2019s been quick to develop new products and adapt old ones for the AI era. Compared with other cybersecurity companies, it also derives a relatively high share of its revenue from software and recurrent revenue rather than hardware and one-time sales. Analysts forecast that its revenue will top $8 billion in the current fiscal year, up 19% from the previous year."}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2023, "rank": 604, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2022, "rank": 666, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2021, "rank": null, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2021, "rank": 682, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2020, "rank": null, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "future", "title": "The Future 50", "year": 2020, "rank": 32, "itemDescription": "<p class=\"p1\">Palo Alto Networks spent the past couple of years knitting together a one-stop cybersecurity shop. The digital defender has spent nearly $3\u00a0billion on acquisitions since CEO Nikesh Arora took the helm in June 2018. As the pandemic pushes workers remote, Palo Alto is capitalizing on the trend by helping companies protect assets outside the traditional corporate firewall. Investors have apparently forgiven the company for being unprofitable on a GAAP basis as total shareholder returns averaged 19% over the past three years through Sept.\u00a030.</p>"}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2020, "rank": 804, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2019, "rank": 912, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "future", "title": "The Future 50", "year": 2017, "rank": 15, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "change-the-world", "title": "Change the World", "year": 2017, "rank": 49, "itemDescription": ""}]}